oneOf.description:
Where on the default branch the pipeline is declared, and the commit that last applied it;
absent for a pipeline defined in the web app before pipeline files, which is read-only until
`gitmoap pipelines export` writes it as a file.

UpdateAnchorRequest.description:
What may change about an anchor after it is pinned: whether the platform keeps following it, and
what deleting its code should mean. The region and the commit it was pinned at cannot change;
pin a new anchor instead.

post.description:
Sets whether the publisher is verified, which is what lets it publish verified-tier providers;
while it is not, none of its submissions can be approved. Platform administrators only.

**Required scope:** `provider_admin:manage`

Takes `ai_org.approve` and a person. Only a revision in the `proposed` state can be declined;
anything else is a conflict.

**Required scope:** `ai_org:approve`

get.description:
The values this group sets itself, each absent where the group leaves it to the groups above;
nothing is filled in from them. Anyone who can see the group may read them.

**Required scope:** `mr_settings:read`

post.description:
The response names a TXT record (`_gitmoap-challenge.<domain>`) and the value to publish in it;
verify the domain once the record is live. A claim holds nothing until it is verified, so
several organizations may claim the same domain and the first to publish its record owns it;
claiming a domain another organization has verified is refused, and claiming your own again
returns the same challenge. Only the organization's owners claim domains.

**Required scope:** `sso:manage`

delete.description:
Removes the repository's own fork settings, so every field is inherited again. Maintainers may;
when what it inherits would let the repository be forked outside its organization, an Owner.

**Required scope:** `repo:update`

get.description:
The merge request's history, newest first: opened, edited, closed, reopened and merged;
reviewers added and removed, approvals, rejections and their withdrawal; commits pushed;
threads resolved and reopened; and stack retargets and restacks — each with who did it and
when. Anyone who can read the merge request may call it. Page with `cursor`.

**Required scope:** `merge_request:read`
